          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"}> fundamental groupoid
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} fundamental ∞-groupoid
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+category"}> fundamental category
                ,
                %strong
                  fundamental
                  = succeed "-category" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn ∞
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %h2#references References
          %p The above definition, evident as it may be, does not seem to be in the literature. Articles that do discuss proposals for aspects of fundamental higher categories of directed spaces include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Grandis"}> Marco Grandis
                ,
                %em Directed homotopy theory, I. The fundamental category
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.AT/0111048"}> arXiv
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Porter"}> Tim Porter
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Enriched categories and models for spaces of evolving states
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Porter"}> Tim Porter
                ,
                %em Enriched categories and models for spaces of
              %p
                dipaths. A discussion document and overview of some techniques_ (
                %a{:href => "http://drops.dagstuhl.de/opus/volltexte/2007/898/pdf/06341.PorterTimothy.Paper.898.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicially+enriched+categories"} simplicially enriched categories
            obtained from a directed space according to the latter have as morphisms directed paths, and as 2-morphisms
            %em directed homotopies
            (paths of directed paths). This is in contrast to the above definition, where the 2-cells away from their boundary are unconstrained.
          %p
            Accordingly it seems that the definition by Porter produces indeed not a Kan-complex enriched category (a model for an
            = succeed "-category)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C2%29-categories"} (∞,2)-categories
            %em
              fundamental
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,2)
            of a space. Generally, in the fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,n)-category
            the
            = succeed "-cells" do
